## Addendum: How to actually wield HTML, start to finish

You don't need an IDE, a compiler, or any developer tools. HTML is just text. If you can save a note, you can run a webpage. Here's the whole path, tablet-friendly.

**1. Get the code.**
When an AI gives you HTML — either as a file you can download directly, or as a block of text in the conversation — that's the entire program. There's nothing else to install. If it's already handed to you as a downloadable file, skip to step 4.

**2. If it's given as text instead of a file, get a plain text editor.**
Not a word processor — Notes, Word, and Google Docs add hidden formatting that breaks code. You need something that saves plain text with no formatting: on a phone or tablet, apps like Notepad, iA Writer, Textastic, or a "Plain Text" mode in your notes app all work. This was the exact wall hit early in this project — the fix is a dedicated plain-text app, once, and it's solved permanently.

**3. Paste the code in, and save it.**
Copy everything the AI gave you — the whole block, starting from `<!DOCTYPE html>` down to the final `</html>` — and paste it into the plain text app. Save it.

**4. Name the file correctly.**
This is the one step that trips people up: the file has to end in `.html`, not `.txt`. Most text apps default to `.txt`. You need to rename the file (or set the extension when saving) to something like `my-module.html`. On most phones and tablets, this happens from the Files app — long-press the file, choose Rename, change the ending. That extension is the only thing telling your device "open this like a webpage, not like a note."

**5. Open it.**
Tap the `.html` file (or open it from your Files app) and it should open directly in your browser, running the whole thing — no internet required after the download, no install, no account. If it opens as plain text instead of running, the extension usually wasn't actually changed to `.html` — back to step 4.

**6. Test it like a real user, not like the person who built it.**
Play through it the way someone who's never seen it would. This is exactly how the answer-position bug in this project got caught — not by reading code, by actually playing the thing several times and noticing a pattern. No code-reading required to catch a real problem. Just honest use.

**7. Report what you actually saw, specifically, and get a fixed file back.**
Describe the exact thing that felt off — not "something's wrong," but what actually happened. Get an updated file back, save it over the old one (or as a new version), test again.

That's the entire loop, forever: get the file, name it right, open it, play it honestly, report what's actually true, get it fixed. No coding required anywhere in that chain — the only real skill is naming a file correctly and describing what was actually noticed.

---

## The short version

Know what you want. Know what you don't want. Name the outcome, not the mechanism. Hold a rough vision and check decisions against it. Report specific problems, not vague unease. Ask for verification when it matters. Save the state before you run out of room. Treat it as two-sided work.

That's the whole method. Everything else is just this, applied to a specific project.
